defects.correct_threshold:
`f64_gt(r_val, F64_TINY)` (error.cyr's DBL_MIN, equivalently r_val != 0) — the quotients are bounded by 1 by construction, so zero is the only thing that can fail. But the threshold is NOT the whole repair, and shipping it alone is measurably worthless:
 (a) line 536's `f64_abs(hii) > EPSILON_F64` tests the SAME quantity (line 507 stores H[j][j] = r_val) against the same absolute epsilon and must move with it, or x stays wrong at every scale where 499 fires;
 (b) line 471's `wj_norm > EPSILON_F64` is the same class in the same loop and binds FIRST (it, not 499, is what breaks p = -42);
 (c) the else-branch must stop fabricating convergence: at a genuinely zero column the minimum residual over the Krylov space is unchanged, so g[j+1] must carry |g[j]| forward rather than being zeroed — otherwise the singular fixture in `why` (2) still reports convergence with a 100% residual, identically before and after the threshold change.
Only all three together restore bit-exact scale covariance (measured: p ∈ [-500, +500], 13 scales, x bit-identical, 3 matvecs at every one). Caveat outside the window: r_val is computed as a naive sum of squares, so it is a FALSE zero once |hjj|, |hj1j| < ~1.5e-154 (their squares underflow); below p ≈ -513 a DBL_MIN threshold is unreachable and only a hypot-style computation would help.
